That’s a common analogy, and it captures one possibility well.

The “dial-up internet” era wasn’t just about slow speeds. It was a period where people hadn’t yet discovered most of the applications that broadband, smartphones, cloud computing, and social media would eventually enable. The infrastructure existed in an early form, but the ecosystem hadn’t compounded.

The same could be true of AI. Improvements may come not just from better models, but from several reinforcing feedback loops:

* Better models help people create better tools.
* Better tools help people generate better data and software.
* Better software accelerates research and engineering.
* That research produces better models.

If those loops remain productive, capabilities can increase faster than any single improvement would suggest.

Your “compounding interest” metaphor fits that dynamic. In finance, growth accelerates because each gain becomes part of the principal.

The caution is that compounding isn’t guaranteed to continue indefinitely. Real systems encounter bottlenecks—compute, energy, economics, regulation, data quality, or diminishing returns. Sometimes progress follows an S-curve: rapid growth after an inflection point, then slowing as constraints become more important.

Still, if we’re early in the adoption curve, history suggests the biggest changes often come from the surrounding ecosystem rather than from raw capability alone. The internet became transformative when networking, browsers, search, payments, mobile devices, and developer platforms all reinforced one another. AI may follow a similar pattern if its complementary technologies continue to mature together.